>
<p>Hire a website audit consultant when a complex site question needs independent investigation and judgement. Define the decision, evidence, deliverables and limits before comparing providers.</p>
<p>A consultant is useful for a migration, unexplained decline, disputed finding or cross-functional backlog. A self-serve audit may suit a small site with an experienced in-house reviewer.</p>

<h2 id="when-you-need-a-consultant">When do you need a website audit consultant?</h2>
<p>You need a consultant when the cost of a wrong diagnosis exceeds the cost of independent review. The consultant should test hypotheses rather than promise a preferred explanation.</p>
<ul>
  <li>Use consultation before a domain, platform or URL migration.</li>
  <li>Use consultation when search, performance and user evidence point in different directions.</li>
  <li>Use consultation when stakeholders need one documented priority order.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="what-they-do">What should a website audit consultant do?</h2>
<p>A consultant should define scope, collect permitted evidence, validate observations and deliver a prioritised plan. Each finding needs an affected URL or template and an observable verification step.</p>
<p>Google's <a href="https://developers.google.com/search/docs/crawling-indexing">crawling and indexing documentation</a> shows why a crawl result, robots rule and indexing status answer different questions. A consultant should keep those signals separate.</p>

<h2 id="freelancer-vs-agency">Should you choose a freelancer or agency?</h2>
<p>Choose based on delivery risk and expertise rather than the label. A freelancer can offer one direct contact.</p>
<p>An agency can offer reviewer coverage and a broader team. Ask who will perform the work in either case.</p>
<div class="overflow-x-auto" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Freelance consultant and agency comparison">
<table><thead><tr><th>Question</th><th>Freelancer</th><th>Agency</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Who performs the analysis?</td><td>Usually one named person</td><td>Confirm analyst and reviewer</td></tr>
<tr><td>How is absence covered?</td><td>Ask before kickoff</td><td>Ask about team continuity</td></tr>
<tr><td>How is quality checked?</td><td>Request method and sample</td><td>Request review process and sample</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>

<h2 id="how-to-evaluate">How should you evaluate a consultant?</h2>
<p>Evaluate a provider through a sample, method and concrete scope. A strong sample records evidence, limits and verification instead of generic advice.</p>
<ul>
  <li>Ask which pages, systems and data sources the quote covers.</li>
  <li>Ask how tool warnings are manually validated.</li>
  <li>Ask what the final files contain and who owns implementation.</li>
  <li>Ask how confidential exports and access are handled.</li>
</ul>
<p>Use the <a href="/website-audit-services/report-example/">AuditWeb report example</a> as a practical comparison format.</p>

<h2 id="red-flags">Which audit-consultant claims are red flags?</h2>
<p>Avoid providers that guarantee rankings, hide the method or sell an instant tool export as a manual investigation. Treat unverified client counts and outcome statistics as marketing claims rather than evidence.</p>
<p>Google states that crawling and indexing cannot be guaranteed in its <a href="https://developers.google.com/search/help/crawling-index-faq">crawling and indexing FAQ</a>. A consultant cannot guarantee an outcome that the search engine itself does not guarantee.</p>

<h2 id="cost-range">How should you compare consultant cost and scope?</h2>
<p>Compare the price against the work described. Site size, template variety, data preparation, manual tests and deliverable formats all affect effort.</p>
<p>AuditWeb's basic audit starts at $297 USD and its comprehensive audit starts at $997 USD. Consulting, migrations, large sites and ongoing work use a custom quote.</p>

<h2 id="working-with-us">What does AuditWeb offer?</h2>
<p>AuditWeb offers a remote audit service with a written scope, evidence-based findings and verification steps. The quote confirms who reviews the site, required access, deliverables, price and delivery date.</p>
<p>Implementation and ongoing monitoring require a separate scope. Use the form with your site URL, main question and priority pages to request an appropriate option.</p>
